  number   =  2,
  pages    = "521--530",
  abstract = "Reactive oxygen species - superoxide, hydrogen peroxide and
              hydroxyl radicals - have long been suspected of constraining
              bacterial growth in important microbial habitats and indeed of
              shaping microbial communities. Over recent decades, studies of
              paradigmatic organisms such as Escherichia coli, Salmonella
              typhimurium, Bacillus subtilis and Saccharomyces cerevisiae have
              pinpointed the biomolecules that oxidants can damage and the
              strategies by which microbes minimize their injuries. What is
              lacking is a good sense of the circumstances under which oxidative
              stress actually occurs. In this MiniReview several potential
              natural sources of oxidative stress are considered: endogenous ROS
              formation, chemical oxidation of reduced species at oxic-anoxic
              interfaces, H2 O2 production by lactic acid bacteria, the
