  pages     = "145--157.e13",
  abstract  = "SummaryContrary to multicellular organisms that display
               segmentation during development, communities of unicellular
               organisms are believed to be devoid of such sophisticated
               patterning. Unexpectedly, we find that the gene expression
               underlying the nitrogen stress response of a developing Bacillus
               subtilis biofilm becomes organized into a ring-like pattern.
               Mathematical modeling and genetic probing of the underlying
               circuit indicate that this patterning is generated by a clock and
               wavefront mechanism, similar to that driving vertebrate
               somitogenesis. We experimentally validated this hypothesis by
               showing that predicted nutrient conditions can even lead to
               multiple concentric rings, resembling segments. We additionally
               confirmed that this patterning mechanism is driven by
               cell-autonomous oscillations. Importantly, we show that the clock
               and wavefront process also spatially patterns sporulation within
