              and metabonomic analyses to uncover syntrophic cooperation between
              inoculant (Bacillus velezensis SQR9) and plant-beneficial
              indigenous Pseudomonas stutzeri in the cucumber rhizosphere. We
              found that the synergistic interaction of these two species is
              highly environmental dependent, the emergence of syntrophic
              cooperation was only evident in a static nutrient-rich niche, such
              as pellicle biofilm in addition to the rhizosphere. Our results
              identified branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs) biosynthesis
              pathways are involved in syntrophic cooperation. Genome-scale
              metabolic modeling and metabolic profiling also demonstrated
