  volume    =  380,
  number    =  3,
  pages     = "467--471",
  abstract  = "Currently there is pressing need to develop novel therapeutic
               agents for the treatment of infections by the human respiratory
               pathogens Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Streptococcus pneumoniae.
               The neuraminidases of these pathogens are important for host
               colonization in animal models of infection and are attractive
               targets for drug discovery. To aid in the development of
