  journal  = "Biofilms",
  volume   =  3,
  pages    =  100056,
  abstract = "Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterial pathogen associated with a
              wide range of infections and utilizes several strategies to
              establish and maintain infection including biofilm production,
              multidrug resistance, and antibiotic tolerance. Multidrug
              resistance in P. aeruginosa, as well as in all other bacterial
              pathogens, is a growing concern. Aminoglycoside resistance, in
              particular, is a major concern in P. aeruginosa infections and
              must be better understood in order to maintain effective clinical
              treatment. In this review, the various antibiotic resistance and
              tolerance mechanisms of Pseudomonas are explored including:
              classic mutation driven resistance, adaptive resistance, persister
              cells, small colony variants, phoenix colonies, and biofilms. It
