               retraction and signal transduction through the Chp chemosensory
               system, a chemotaxis-like sensory system that regulates cAMP
               production and transcription of hundreds of genes, including key
               virulence factors. Like other chemotaxis pathways, pili-mediated
               surface sensing results in a transient response amplified by a
               positive feedback that increases type IV pili activity, thereby
               promoting long-term surface attachment that can stimulate
               additional virulence and biofilm-inducing pathways. The
               methyl-accepting chemotaxis protein-like chemosensor PilJ
               directly interacts with the major pilin subunit PilA. Our results
               thus support a mechanochemical model where a chemosensory system
               measures the mechanically induced conformational changes in
               stretched type IV pili. These findings demonstrate that P.
               aeruginosa not only uses type IV pili for surface-specific
               twitching motility, but also as a sensor regulating
               surface-induced gene expression and pathogenicity.",
  month     =  jun,
  year      =  2015,
  language  = "en"
}

@ARTICLE{Leighton2015-zt,
