  journal  = "Front. Microbiol.",
  volume   =  5,
  pages    =  554,
  abstract = "Chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa lung infection is the most severe
              complication in patients with cystic fibrosis (CF). The infection
              is characterized by the formation of biofilm surrounded by
              numerous polymorphonuclear leukocytes (PMNs) and strong O2
              depletion in the endobronchial mucus. We have reported that O2 is
              mainly consumed by the activated PMNs, while O2 consumption by
              aerobic respiration is diminutive and nitrous oxide (N2O) is
              produced in infected CF sputum. This suggests that the reported
              growth rates of P. aeruginosa in lungs and sputum may result from
              anaerobic respiration using denitrification. The growth rate of P.
              aeruginosa achieved by denitrification at physiological levels
              (~400 μM) of nitrate (NO(-) 3) is however, not known. Therefore,
              we have measured growth rates of anoxic cultures of PAO1 and
              clinical isolates (n = 12) in LB media supplemented with NO(-) 3
