              increase in activation and expression and rapid consumption of
              NO(-) 3 followed by a transient increase of NO(-) 2. Growth rates
              obtained by denitrification in this study were comparable to our
              reported growth rates in the majority of P. aeruginosa cells in CF
              lungs and sputum. Thus, we have demonstrated that denitrification
              is required for P. aeruginosa growth in infected endobronchial CF
              mucus.",
  month    =  oct,
  year     =  2014,
  keywords = "Pseudomonas aeruginosa; cystic fibrosis; denitrification; growth",
  language = "en"
}

@ARTICLE{Ruff2015-wr,
  title    = "Autoimmune host-microbiota interactions at barrier sites and
              beyond",
  author   = "Ruff, William E and Kriegel, Martin A",
  journal  = "Trends Mol. Med.",
  volume   =  21,
  number   =  4,
  pages    = "233--244",
  abstract = "The microbiota is considered to be an important factor influencing
              the pathogenesis of autoimmunity at both barrier sites and
              internal organs. Impinging on innate and adaptive immunity,
              commensals exert protective or detrimental effects on various
              autoimmune animal models. Human microbiome studies of autoimmunity
              remain largely descriptive, but suggest a role for dysbiosis in
              autoimmune disease. Humanized gnotobiotic approaches have advanced
              our understanding of immune-commensal interactions, but little is
