  journal  = "NPJ Biofilms Microbiomes",
  volume   =  7,
  number   =  1,
  pages    =  22,
  abstract = "Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a ubiquitous opportunistic pathogen that
              forms robust biofilms in the different niches it occupies.
              Numerous physiological adaptations are required as this organism
              shifts from soil or aquatic environments to a host-associated
              lifestyle. While many conditions differ between these niches,
              temperature shifts are a factor that can contribute to
              physiological stress during this transition. To understand how
              temperature impacts biofilm formation in this pathogen, we used
