  volume    =  114,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "131--135",
  abstract  = "CRISPR-Cas are prokaryotic adaptive immune systems that provide
               protection against bacteriophage (phage) and other parasites.
               Little is known about how CRISPR-Cas systems are regulated,
               preventing prediction of phage dynamics in nature and
               manipulation of phage resistance in clinical settings. Here, we
               show that the bacterium Pseudomonas aeruginosa PA14 uses the
               cell–cell communication process, called quorum sensing, to
               activate cas gene expression, to increase CRISPR-Cas targeting of
               foreign DNA, and to promote CRISPR adaptation, all at high cell
