  booktitle = "Metabolism and Respiration",
  publisher = "Academic Press",
  pages     = "525--579",
  abstract  = "Publisher Summary This chapter presents evidences to show that
               dark respiration and photorespiration are different processes.
               Dark respiration is defined as CO2 evolution or O2 uptake
               resulting from the oxidation of substrates via the routes of
               glycolysis, the oxidative pentose phosphate pathway, and the
               tricarboxylic acid pathway linked to oxidative electron transport
               pathways of the mitochondrion. It may occur in the dark or in the
               light. Photorespiration is defined as the light-dependent
               oxidation of a C2 metabolite derived from photosynthesis. Dark
               respiration and photorespiration are distinct processes having
               separate metabolic pathways, physiological responses, and, in
               part, locations within the green cell. The substrate for
               photorespiration is derived from photosynthesis and is probably,
               mainly, glycolate that is oxidized by the C2 pathway. The
               physiological, as opposed to biochemical, distinction between
