              frequently involves two-component systems in which a sensor
              histidine kinase (HK) autophosphorylates in response to a stimulus
              before subsequently transferring the phosphoryl group to a
              response regulator that controls downstream effectors. Many
              details of the molecular mechanisms of HK activation are still
              unclear due to complications associated with the multiple
              signaling states of these large, multidomain proteins. To address
              these challenges, we combined complementary solution biophysical
              approaches to examine the conformational changes upon activation
              of a minimal, blue-light-sensing histidine kinase from
              Erythrobacter litoralis HTCC2594, EL346. Our data show that
              multiple conformations coexist in the dark state of EL346 in
              solution, which may explain the enzyme's residual dark-state
              activity. We also observe that activation involves destabilization
              of the helices in the dimerization and histidine
              phosphotransfer-like domain, where the phosphoacceptor histidine
              resides, and their interactions with the catalytic domain. Similar
