              introduction of a heterologous electron transfer pathway. E. coli
              expressing mtrCAB from Shewanella oneidensis MR-1 consumed
              electrons directly from a cathode when fumarate or nitrate, both
              intracellular electron acceptors, were present. The
              fumarate-triggered current consumption occurred only when fumarate
              reductase was present, indicating all the electrons passed through
              this enzyme. Moreover, MtrCAB-expressing E. coli used current to
              stoichiometrically produce ammonia. Thus, our work introduces a
              modular genetic tool to reduce a specific intracellular redox
              molecule with an electrode, opening the possibility of
