  volume   =  72,
  pages    = "309--330",
  abstract = "2017 marks the 60th anniversary of Krebs' seminal paper on the
              glyoxylate shunt (and coincidentally, also the 80th anniversary of
              his discovery of the citric acid cycle). Sixty years on, we have
              witnessed substantial developments in our understanding of how
              flux is partitioned between the glyoxylate shunt and the oxidative
              decarboxylation steps of the citric acid cycle. The last decade
              has shown us that the beautifully elegant textbook mechanism that
              regulates carbon flux through the shunt in E. coli is an
              oversimplification of the situation in many other bacteria. The
              aim of this review is to assess how this new knowledge is
