  journal  = "Curr. Biol.",
  volume   =  29,
  number   =  11,
  pages    = "R545--R550",
  abstract = "Microbes are social organisms, interacting primarily through
              secreted biomolecules. Many traits have evolved based solely on
              their effects upon other community members, yet even individually
              beneficial traits often create social side effects that are
              mediated by spatial population structure. Predicting the evolution
              of many microbial traits thus requires a comprehensive
              understanding of their social consequences. In this review, we
              examine the critical role of population spatial structure in
              microbial social evolution. We briefly review key mechanisms
              structuring microbial communities, focusing primarily on the
              universal roles of cellular death and reproduction. Finally, we
              explain how spatial assortment can be efficiently calculated in
