  abstract = "Oxygen transfer agents have long been sought as a means to treat
              hypoxia caused by congenital or acquired conditions.
              Hemoglobin-based oxygen carriers were in clinical development as
              blood substitutes, but development was halted due to the finding
              of significant vasoactivity. Rather than develop a blood
              substitute, a product for indications characterized by hypoxia is
              in development. PEGylated bovine carboxyhemoglobin (SANGUINATE™)
              is both a carbon monoxide releasing molecule and an oxygen
              transfer agent. It is comprised of three functional components
              that act to inhibit vasoconstriction, reduce inflammation and
              optimize the delivery of oxygen. SANGUINATE has the potential to
              reduce or prevent the effects of ischemia by inhibiting
              vasoconstriction and re-oxygenating tissue. Phase 1 safety trials
