              including electron microscopy, immunohistochemistry or
              immunofluorescence using bacteria-specific antibodies, and
              fluorescent in situ hybridization (FISH) using a
              fluorescence-labeled oligonucleotide probe. Nevertheless, these
              methods are not widely used due to technical limitation or
              difficulties. Here a method to localize bacteria within
              paraffin-embedded tissues using DIG-labeled DNA probes has been
              introduced. The paraffin-embedded tissues are the most common form
              of biopsy tissues available from pathology banks. Bacteria can be
              detected either in a species-specific or universal manner.
              Bacterial signals are detected as either discrete forms (coccus,
              rod, fusiform, and hairy form) of bacteria or dispersed forms. The
