  volume    =  6,
  pages     = "e19599",
  abstract  = "When pathogens enter the host, sensing of environmental cues
               activates the expression of virulence genes. Opposite transition
               of pathogens from activating to non-activating conditions is
               poorly understood. Interestingly, variability in the expression
               of virulence genes upon infection enhances colonization. In order
               to systematically detect the role of phenotypic variability in
               enteropathogenic E. coli (EPEC), an important human pathogen,
               both in virulence activating and non-activating conditions, we
               employed the ScanLag methodology. The analysis revealed a bimodal
               growth rate. Mathematical modeling combined with experimental
               analysis showed that this bimodality is mediated by a hysteretic
               memory-switch that results in the stable co-existence of
               non-virulent and hyper-virulent subpopulations, even after many
               generations of growth in non-activating conditions. We identified
               the per operon as the key component of the hysteretic switch.
               This unique hysteretic memory switch may result in persistent
               infection and enhanced host-to-host spreading.",
