  journal  = "Science",
  volume   =  354,
  number   =  6314,
  pages    = "886--889",
  abstract = "Plants are responsive to temperature, and some species can
              distinguish differences of 1°C. In Arabidopsis, warmer temperature
              accelerates flowering and increases elongation growth
              (thermomorphogenesis). However, the mechanisms of temperature
              perception are largely unknown. We describe a major thermosensory
              role for the phytochromes (red light receptors) during the night.
              Phytochrome null plants display a constitutive warm-temperature
              response, and consistent with this, we show in this background
              that the warm-temperature transcriptome becomes derepressed at low
              temperatures. We found that phytochrome B (phyB) directly
              associates with the promoters of key target genes in a
