  volume    =  541,
  number    =  7637,
  pages     = "353--358",
  abstract  = "The origin and cellular complexity of eukaryotes represent a
               major enigma in biology. Current data support scenarios in which
               an archaeal host cell and an alphaproteobacterial (mitochondrial)
               endosymbiont merged together, resulting in the first eukaryotic
               cell. The host cell is related to Lokiarchaeota, an archaeal
               phylum with many eukaryotic features. The emergence of the
               structural complexity that characterizes eukaryotic cells remains
               unclear. Here we describe the 'Asgard' superphylum, a group of
               uncultivated archaea that, as well as Lokiarchaeota, includes
