              mortality worldwide. Although Enterobacteriaceae often comprise
              less than 1\% of a healthy intestine's microbiota, some of these
              organisms can bloom in the inflamed gut; expansion of
              enterobacteria is a hallmark of microbial imbalance known as
              dysbiosis. Microcins are small secreted proteins that possess
              antimicrobial activity in vitro, but whose role in vivo has been
              unclear. Here we demonstrate that microcins enable the probiotic
              bacterium Escherichia coli Nissle 1917 (EcN) to limit the
              expansion of competing Enterobacteriaceae (including pathogens and
              pathobionts) during intestinal inflammation. Microcin-producing
              EcN limits the growth of competitors in the inflamed intestine,
              including commensal E. coli, adherent-invasive E. coli and the
              related pathogen Salmonella enterica. Moreover, only therapeutic
              administration of the wild-type, microcin-producing EcN to mice
