  volume    =  535,
  number    =  7613,
  pages     = "511--516",
  abstract  = "The vast majority of systemic bacterial infections are caused by
               facultative, often antibiotic-resistant, pathogens colonizing
               human body surfaces. Nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus
               predisposes to invasive infection, but the mechanisms that permit
               or interfere with pathogen colonization are largely unknown.
               Whereas soil microbes are known to compete by production of
               antibiotics, such processes have rarely been reported for human
               microbiota. We show that nasal Staphylococcus lugdunensis strains
               produce lugdunin, a novel thiazolidine-containing cyclic peptide
               antibiotic that prohibits colonization by S. aureus, and a rare
