  journal  = "Science",
  volume   =  352,
  number   =  6289,
  pages    = "1098--1101",
  abstract = "Ninety percent of lignocellulose-degrading fungi contain genes
              encoding lytic polysaccharide monooxygenases (LPMOs). These
              enzymes catalyze the initial oxidative cleavage of recalcitrant
              polysaccharides after activation by an electron donor.
              Understanding the source of electrons is fundamental to fungal
              physiology and will also help with the exploitation of LPMOs for
              biomass processing. Using genome data and biochemical methods, we
