  pages    = "1--39",
  abstract = "Secondary metabolites, including antibiotics, are produced in
              nature and serve survival functions for the organisms producing
              them. The antibiotics are a heterogeneous group, the functions of
              some being related to and others being unrelated to their
              antimicrobial activities. Secondary metabolites serve: (i) as
              competitive weapons used against other bacteria, fungi, amoebae,
              plants, insects, and large animals; (ii) as metal transporting
              agents; (iii) as agents of symbiosis between microbes and plants,
              nematodes, insects, and higher animals; (iv) as sexual hormones;
