  volume   =  32,
  number   =  4,
  pages    = "271--280",
  abstract = "Despite thermodynamic, bioenergetic and phylogenetic failings, the
              81-year-old concept of primordial soup remains central to
              mainstream thinking on the origin of life. But soup is homogeneous
              in pH and redox potential, and so has no capacity for energy
              coupling by chemiosmosis. Thermodynamic constraints make
              chemiosmosis strictly necessary for carbon and energy metabolism
              in all free-living chemotrophs, and presumably the first
              free-living cells too. Proton gradients form naturally at alkaline
              hydrothermal vents and are viewed as central to the origin of
              life. Here we consider how the earliest cells might have harnessed
              a geochemically created proton-motive force and then learned to
              make their own, a transition that was necessary for their escape
