@MISC{Kram2011-cw,
  title    = "No Title",
  author   = "Kram, Karin E",
  address  = "Columbia University",
  abstract = "Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans grows as a strongly adherent
              biofilm. The 14-gene tad (tight adherence) locus encodes genes
              necessary for the expression and biogenesis of Flp pili, which are
              required for tenacious adherence. Although studies have been
              concerned with the functions of the Tad proteins, mechanisms of
              regulation of tad locus expression in A. actinomycetemcomitans
              were unknown. Two main types of regulation have been explored in
              this study, (1) cis-acting regulatory elements found within the
              tad locus and (2) trans-acting regulatory mechanisms encoded
              outside the locus. Identification of mechanisms within the locus
              focused on the need of A. actinomycetemcomitans to produce
              abundant pilin protein compared to the other tad proteins. These
              studies resulted in identification of a transcriptional
              termination cascade within the tad locus. Northern blot analysis
              of the tad locus showed two abundant, short transcripts. If I
              assumed that they both started at the known tad promoter, their
              lengths predicted that the most abundant transcript terminated
              just beyond the pilin gene (flp-1). The other transcript was
              predicted to terminate just after tadV, which encodes the protease
              that cleaves the Flp1 prepilin to form mature Flp1 pilin. When I
              examined the nucleotide sequences, the region downstream of tadV
              revealed no obvious element, but the region immediately downstream
              of flp-1 showed a strong stem-loop structure typical of intrinsic
              transcriptional terminators. The stem- loop structure and its
              location were conserved in other strains of A.
              actinomycetemcomitans. A region at the 3‚Äô end of the tad locus
              also resembled an intrinsic terminator. The three regions were
              cloned between the strong tac promoter and Escherichia coli lacZ.
              The region after flp-1 reduced lacZ expression greater than 99\%;
              but the reduction was 0\% if the stem-loop region was deleted. I
              call this terminator T1. The second (T2), after tadV, reduced lacZ
              about 36\%; and the third (T3), about 86\%. T1 and T3 have the
              structures and strengths of intrinsic terminators, but T2 lacks an
              obvious intrinsic terminator. I considered that termination may be
              Rho-dependent. E. coli Rho termination factor is inhibited by
              bicylomycin (BCM), and BCM blocked termination at T2, but not at
              T1 and T3, in agreement with my predictions. I propose that the
              proper stoichiometry of tad gene products is achieved in A.
              actinomycetemcomitans by, at least in part, a transcription
              termination cascade. In Chapter 3, I show that both a rough-colony
              (Tad+) natural isolate and its smooth-colony (Tad-) laboratory
              variant adhere and produce a biofilm. Adherence by the Tad+ strain
              is considerably stronger than that by the isogenic Tad- strain;
              and the structure of the biofilm from the Tad+ strain is very
              different from the biofilm of the Tad- strain, with the latter
              having the properties of a ‚Äúclassic‚Äù biofilm. I chose several
              regulators that I thought might affect expression of the tad locus
              in A. actinomycetemcomitans. Deletion of their genes and the
              resulting strain phenotypes led to cpxR, whose deletion affected
              phenotypes of both the Tad+ and Tad- strains. The adherence of the
              smooth (Tad-) strain was complemented by cpxR in trans, whereas
              the adherence of the rough (Tad+) strain required the presence of
              the next downstream gene, cpxA, for complementation to occur.
              These studies identified trans-acting regulatory genes for the tad
              locus. Furthermore, they show significant differences between
              rough-colony forming (Tad+) and smooth-colony forming (Tad-)
              strains of A. actinomycetemcomitans.",
  year     =  2011
}

