  pages    = "1406--1408",
  abstract = "The production of MG (methylglyoxal) in bacterial cells must be
              maintained in balance with the capacity for detoxification and
              protection against this electrophile. Excessive production of MG
              leads to cell death. Survival of exposure to MG is best understood
              in the Gram-negative bacteria. The major mechanism of protection
              is the spontaneous reaction of MG with GSH to form
              hemithiolacetal, followed by detoxification by the glyoxalase
              system leading to the production of D-lactate. The KefB and KefC
              glutathione-gated K(+) efflux systems are integrated with the
              activity of the glyoxalase system to regulate the cytoplasmic pH
