              since a stable host-parasite ratio is maintained. Free-living
              amoebae represent an important reservoir of ARB and may, while
              encysted, protect the internalized bacteria from chlorine and
              other biocides. Free-living amoebae may act as a Trojan horse,
              bringing hidden ARB within the human ``Troy,'' and may produce
              vesicles filled with ARB, increasing their transmission potential.
              Free-living amoebae may also play a role in the selection of
              virulence traits and in adaptation to survival in macrophages.
              Thus, intra-amoebal growth was found to enhance virulence, and
