  volume    =  2,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "1--7",
  abstract  = "AbstractThe acquisition of antibiotic resistance in bacteria,
               though a deeply concerning international issue, is reasonably
               well-understood at a mechanistic level. Less well-understood is
               why bacteria that are sensitive in vitro to well-established and
               widely-used antibiotics sometimes fail to respond to these agents
               in vivo. This is a particularly common problem in chronic,
               polymicrobial infection scenarios. Here, we discuss this in
               vitro-in vivo disconnect from the perspective of the bacterium,
               focusing in particular on how infection micro/macro-environment,
               biogeography, and the presence of co-habiting species affect the
