  publisher = "American Society for Microbiology1752 N St., N.W., Washington, DC",
  volume    =  207,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "e0042924",
  abstract  = "Bacterial aggregates are observed in both natural and artificial
               environments. In the context of disease, aggregates have been
               isolated from chronic and acute infections. Pseudomonas
               aeruginosa (Pa) aggregates contribute significantly to chronic
               infections, particularly in the lungs of people with cystic
               fibrosis (CF). Unlike the large biofilm structures observed in
