               cells. Many of the mechanisms used by the cell-autonomous innate
               immune system in eukaryotic cells are ancient and have
               evolutionary roots in bacterial systems that defend against phage
               infection. Studies from recent years have shown that modification
               of the free nucleotide pool is central to many of these conserved
               immune mechanisms. In this Review, we explain how immune pathways
               manipulate the available pool of nucleotides to deprive viruses
               of molecules essential for their replication, how immune proteins
               chemically modify nucleotides to generate immune signalling
               molecules, and how cell-autonomous innate immune mechanisms
