               acid was investigated for its in vitro effects on pyoverdine
               production and specific QS-regulated traits of P. aeruginosa.
               Pyoverdine production, fluorescence quenching, QS-related
               virulence traits, and biofilm formation were evaluated using
               wild-type and GFP-expressing biosensor strains. In addition,
               quantitative qRT-PCR was performed to analyse QS and pyoverdine
               gene expressions. Results show that vulpinic acid application
               decreased pyoverdine production by 53.65\% ± 5.45 and quenched
               the produced pyoverdine by 67.55\% ± 2.98. The QS regulated genes
