  journal  = "Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U. S. A.",
  volume   =  98,
  number   =  20,
  pages    = "11621--11626",
  abstract = "Spore formation by the bacterium Bacillus subtilis has long been
              studied as a model for cellular differentiation, but predominantly
              as a single cell. When analyzed within the context of highly
              structured, surface-associated communities (biofilms), spore
              formation was discovered to have heretofore unsuspected spatial
              organization. Initially, motile cells differentiated into aligned
              chains of attached cells that eventually produced aerial
              structures, or fruiting bodies, that served as preferential sites
              for sporulation. Fruiting body formation depended on regulatory
              genes required early in sporulation and on genes evidently needed
              for exopolysaccharide and surfactin production. The formation of
              aerial structures was robust in natural isolates but not in
              laboratory strains, an indication that multicellularity has been
              lost during domestication of B. subtilis. Other microbial
              differentiation processes long thought to involve only single
              cells could display the spatial organization characteristic of
              multicellular organisms when studied with recent natural isolates.",
