  abstract = "Bacteria enjoy an infinite capacity for reproduction as long as
              they reside in an environment supporting growth. However, their
              rapid growth and efficient metabolism ultimately results in
              depletion of growth-supporting substrates and the population of
              cells enters a phase defined as the stationary phase of growth. In
              this phase, their reproductive ability is gradually lost. The
              molecular mechanism underlying this cellular degeneration has not
              been fully deciphered. Still, recent analysis of the physiology
              and molecular biology of stationary-phase E. coli cells has
              revealed interesting similarities to the aging process of higher
              organisms. The similarities include increased oxidation of
