  pages    =  684,
  abstract = "The study of microbial communities often leads to arguments for
              the evolution of cooperation due to group benefits. However,
              multilevel selection models caution against the uncritical
              assumption that group benefits will lead to the evolution of
              cooperation. We analyze a microbial social trait to precisely
              define the conditions favoring cooperation. We combine the
              multilevel partition of the Price equation with a laboratory model
              system: swarming in Pseudomonas aeruginosa. We parameterize a
              population dynamics model using competition experiments where we
