              intermediates. Therefore, an increase in the availability of NADH
              is expected to have an effect on the metabolic distribution. This
              paper investigates a genetic means of manipulating the
              availability of intracellular NADH in vivo by regenerating NADH
              through the heterologous expression of an NAD(+)-dependent formate
              dehydrogenase. More specifically, it explores the effect on the
              metabolic patterns in Escherichia coli under anaerobic and aerobic
              conditions of substituting the native cofactor-independent formate
              dehydrogenase (FDH) by and NAD(+)-dependent FDH from Candida
              boidinii. The over-expression of the NAD(+)-dependent FDH doubled
