              factors, and (5) prevalence of PIs and other blood and body fluid
              exposures. Analyses were directed at determining significant risk
              factors for exposure. RESULTS: Fourteen percent of RNs reported
              one or more PIs in the past 3 years (7.6 per 100 person-years).
              Nearly half (45.8\%) of all PIs were not formally reported. PIs
              were significantly correlated with a number of factors, including
              lack of compliance with Standard Precautions (odds ratio [OR],
              1.72; P=.019; 95\% confidence interval [CI]: 1.09-2.71); recapping
              of needles (OR, 1.78; P=.016; 95\% CI: 1.11-2.86); exposure to
              household stressors (OR, 1.99; P=.005; 95\% CI: 1.22-3.25);
              exposure to violence (OR, 3.47; P=.001; 95\% CI: 1.67-7.20);
