  author   = "Sonnleitner, Elisabeth and Haas, Dieter",
  journal  = "Appl. Microbiol. Biotechnol.",
  volume   =  91,
  number   =  1,
  pages    = "63--79",
  abstract = "Small RNAs (sRNAs) exert important functions in pseudomonads.
              Classical sRNAs comprise the 4.5S, 6S, 10Sa and 10Sb RNAs, which
              are known in enteric bacteria as part of the signal recognition
              particle, a regulatory component of RNA polymerase,
              transfer-messenger RNA (tmRNA) and the RNA component of RNase P,
              respectively. Their homologues in pseudomonads are presumed to
              have analogous functions. Other sRNAs of pseudomonads generally
              have little or no sequence similarity with sRNAs of enteric
              bacteria. Numerous sRNAs repress or activate the translation of
              target mRNAs by a base-pairing mechanism. Examples of this group
              in Pseudomonas aeruginosa are the iron-repressible PrrF1 and PrrF2
              sRNAs, which repress the translation of genes encoding
              iron-containing proteins, and PhrS, an anaerobically inducible
