              human infectious morbidity and mortality. To persist on human
              hosts, S. aureus must have intrinsic defense mechanisms to cope
              with antimicrobial fatty acids, which comprise an important
              component of human innate defense mechanisms. We have identified a
              novel pair of genes, farR and farE, that constitute a dedicated
              regulator and effector of S. aureus resistance to linoleic and
              arachidonic acids, which are major fatty acids in human membrane
              phospholipid. Expression of farE, which encodes an efflux pump, is
              induced in an farR-dependent mechanism, in response to these
