              conditions while, as detailed in a companion article in this
              issue, frequently neglecting to comply with fundamental
              thermodynamic laws. Even if demands from both palaeogeochemistry
              and thermodynamics are respected, then a plethora of strongly
              differing models are still conceivable. Although we have no
              guarantee that life at its origin necessarily resembled biology in
              extant organisms, we consider that the only empirical way to
              deduce how life may have emerged is by taking the stance of
              assuming continuity of biology from its inception to the present
              day. Building upon this conviction, we have assessed extant types
              of energy and carbon metabolism for their appropriateness to
              conditions probably pertaining in those settings of the Hadean
              planet that fulfil the thermodynamic requirements for life to come
              into being. Wood-Ljungdahl (WL) pathways leading to acetyl CoA
              formation are excellent candidates for such primordial metabolism.
