  author   = "Liochev, S I and Fridovich, I",
  journal  = "IUBMB Life",
  volume   =  48,
  number   =  2,
  pages    = "157--161",
  abstract = "Superoxide (O2-) poses multiple threats, which are diminished by a
              family of metalloenzymes, the superoxide dismutases. Among the
              damaging effects of O2- are direct oxidation of
              low-molecular-weight reductants; inactivation of a select group of
              enzymes; and reaction with NO to yield the strong oxidant,
              peroxynitrite. Of even greater import is the ability of O2- to
              univalently oxidize the [4 Fe-4 S] clusters of dehydratases, which
              causes release of iron. The ``free'' iron, which is kept reduced
              by cellular reductants, then reduces hydroperoxides to hydroxyl or
              alkoxyl radicals. Because the ``free'' iron will preferentially
              bind to anionic polymers, such as nucleic acids, or to anionic
              surfaces, such as cell membranes, these radicals will be generated
              adjacent to these vital targets and will preferentially attack
              them. O2- and iron can thus be viewed as partners in crime, and
              reciprocal regulatory effects between iron and O2- may be
              anticipated. These are discussed.",
