              factors. However, unlike E. coli, some bacterial species possess
              multiple FNR proteins that presumably have evolved to fulfill
              distinct roles. Here, three FNR proteins (ANR, PP\_3233, and
              PP\_3287) from a single bacterial species, Pseudomonas putida
              KT2440, have been analyzed. Under anaerobic conditions, all three
              proteins had spectral properties resembling those of [4Fe-4S]
              proteins. The reactivity of the ANR [4Fe-4S] cluster with O2 was
