              clusters reacted more slowly. Transcription from an FNR-dependent
              promoter with a consensus FNR-binding site in P. putida and E.
              coli strains expressing only one FNR protein was consistent with
              the in vitro responses to O2. Taken together, the experimental
              results suggest that the local environments of the iron-sulfur
              clusters in the different P. putida FNR proteins influence their
              reactivity with O2, such that ANR resembles E. coli FNR and is
              highly responsive to low concentrations of O2, whereas PP\_3233
              and PP\_3287 have evolved to be less sensitive to O2.",
  month    =  jul,
  year     =  2015,
  keywords = "bacterial signal transduction bacterial transcription iron-sulfur
